"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"][41]={"university_physics_en:M41-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M41-L1 – Waveguides, Guided Modes and Effective Refractive Index<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M41 – Master: Photonics and Ultrafast Optics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M7: wave optics; M33: lasers and\nphotons; M35: materials physics; M40 · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M40&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Compute a guided-mode effective\nindex from the propagation constant and distinguish phase from group\nvelocity.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>An integrated optical waveguide confines light in a guided mode\nthrough a transverse refractive-index profile. Beyond ray sketches, one\nsolves an electromagnetic boundary-value problem: field profiles have\ntransverse modal structure and associated propagation constants. A high\ncore index by itself does not guarantee single-mode operation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>A locally monochromatic mode carries a longitudinal phase factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mi>β<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exp(i\\beta z-i\\omega t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith vacuum wavenumber\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_0=2\\pi/\\lambda_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ndefine the effective phase index\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_{\\mathrm{eff}}=\\beta/k_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a conventional lossless index-guided mode,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>&lt;<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>&lt;<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_{\\mathrm{clad}}&lt;n_{\\mathrm{eff}}&lt;n_{\\mathrm{core}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBy contrast, the group index is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>β<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_g=c\\,d\\beta/d\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand need not equal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_{\\mathrm{eff}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin a dispersive guide.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>β<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>c<\/mi><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta=\\frac{2\\pi n_{\\mathrm{eff}}}{\\lambda_0},\\qquad v_{\\mathrm{ph}}=\\frac{\\omega}{\\beta}=\\frac{c}{n_{\\mathrm{eff}}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For an illustrative mode at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.55<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_0=1.55\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.70<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_{\\mathrm{eff}}=1.70<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\none obtains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.89<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta=6.89\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_{\\mathrm{core}}=2.0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.45<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_{\\mathrm{clad}}=1.45<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthis lies inside the expected index range. The axial phase period is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.912<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_0/n_{\\mathrm{eff}}\\approx0.912\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure gives a schematic modal field, not a numerical eigenmode\nsolution.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_315_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic planar core with a guided electric-field profile and contrasting refractive indices.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic planar core with a guided\nelectric-field profile and contrasting refractive indices.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Original schematic cross section.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Not an eigenmode solution for a real device.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Compute\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>β<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.50<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_{\\mathrm{eff}}=1.50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat the same vacuum wavelength.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does phase velocity change as effective index rises?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why cannot group index be determined from a single phase-index\nmeasurement?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1.50<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>1.55<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>6.08<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta=2\\pi(1.50)/1.55\\approx6.08\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{\\mathrm{ph}}=c/n_{\\mathrm{eff}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndecreases.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The group index requires the frequency derivative of the propagation\nconstant.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The index bounds refer to the simple lossless guiding model. Leaky\nmodes, complex material constants, anisotropy, metamaterials and strong\ndispersion require more complete treatment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Ring resonators exploit such guided modes and their\nfrequency-dependent phase. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M41&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-46-photonic-materials-and-devices-spring-2006/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OCW – Photonic Materials and Devices: Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M41-L1 – Waveguides, Guided Modes and Effective Refractive Index · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-369-mathematical-methods-in-nanophotonics-spring-2008/pages/readings/\">MIT OCW – Mathematical Methods in Nanophotonics: Readings<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M41-L1 – Waveguides, Guided Modes and Effective Refractive Index · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M41-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M41-L2 – Ring Resonators, Free Spectral Range and Quality Factor<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M41 – Master: Photonics and Ultrafast Optics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M41-L1; M7: interference and\nresonance.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate resonance spacing and\nquality factor from round-trip length, group index and linewidth.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>In a ring resonator, a guided mode returns a complex field amplitude\nafter each round trip. Constructive phase accumulation creates discrete\nresonances. Two observables are distinct: free spectral range (FSR) is\nthe spacing between adjacent resonances, while linewidth describes an\nindividual resonance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For round-trip length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=2\\pi R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe resonance condition is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta(\\omega_m)L=2\\pi m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDifferentiation with respect to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives the local spacing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\nu\\approx c/(n_gL)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe <strong>group index<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>β<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_g=c\\,d\\beta/d\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nincorporates dispersion. For small wavelength spacing,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><msubsup><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\lambda\\approx\\lambda_0^2/(n_gL)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA narrow isolated resonance has loaded quality factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q\\approx\\lambda_0/\\Delta\\lambda_{\\mathrm{FWHM}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\ncoupling affects this value.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≃<\/mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>≃<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\lambda_{\\mathrm{FSR}}\\simeq\\frac{\\lambda_0^2}{n_g\\,2\\pi R},\\qquad Q\\simeq\\frac{\\lambda_0}{\\Delta\\lambda_{\\mathrm{FWHM}}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=10\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_g=4.0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.55<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_0=1.55\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>9.56<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\lambda_{\\mathrm{FSR}}\\approx9.56\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a measured FWHM of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.80<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.80\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\none obtains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1550<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.80<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1938<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q\\approx1550/0.80=1938<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram distinguishes round-trip geometry, resonance spacing and\nlinewidth. These are synthetic values, not vendor loss figures.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_249_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Ring waveguide with an optical bus and separate schematic resonance spectrum.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Ring waveguide with an optical bus and\nseparate schematic resonance spectrum.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Original teaching diagram; FSR and FWHM have distinct\nscales.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\lambda_{\\mathrm{FSR}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen radius is doubled?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why must FSR calculations include dispersion?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\lambda_{\\mathrm{FWHM}}=0.40\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">With all other parameters fixed, FSR halves to about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.78<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4.78\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Adjacent-mode spacing is set by the frequency derivative of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>β<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnot the phase index alone.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1550<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.40<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3875<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=1550/0.40\\approx3875<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The approximation assumes locally slowly varying group index, spacing\nsmall compared with wavelength and a well-isolated line shape. Thermal\ndrift, backscattering, mode splitting and complex coupling complicate\nactual spectra.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Resonators show how material dispersion links optical time and\nfrequency scales. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M41&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-46-photonic-materials-and-devices-spring-2006/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OCW – Photonic Materials and Devices: Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M41-L2 – Ring Resonators, Free Spectral Range and Quality Factor · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-369-mathematical-methods-in-nanophotonics-spring-2008/pages/readings/\">MIT OCW – Mathematical Methods in Nanophotonics: Readings<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M41-L2 – Ring Resonators, Free Spectral Range and Quality Factor · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://optics.ansys.com/hc/en-us/articles/360042800293-Ring-resonator-getting-started-Design-and-initial-simulation\">Ansys Optics – Photonics: Ring Resonator Modeling (Ansys Optics)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M41-L2 – Ring Resonators, Free Spectral Range and Quality Factor · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M41-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M41-L3 – Ultrashort Pulses, Time–Bandwidth and Dispersion<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M41 – Master: Photonics and Ultrafast Optics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M41-L2; M7: Fourier optics; M31:\nnumerical Fourier methods.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Compute the minimum spectral\nbandwidth of a transform-limited Gaussian pulse.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>A short light pulse is not a single monochromatic oscillation. Its\nenvelope results from coherent superposition of frequency components.\nFor a specified pulse shape, shorter temporal duration requires wider\nspectral bandwidth. Spectral phase may broaden the pulse without\nchanging the spectral-intensity bandwidth.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For a transform-limited, unchirped Gaussian pulse, the product of\nintensity full widths at half maximum in time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand ordinary frequency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\nu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.441<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\ln 2/\\pi\\approx0.441<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe coefficient does <strong>not<\/strong> apply to arbitrary pulse\nshapes or RMS widths. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>≪<\/mo><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\lambda\\ll\\lambda_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><msubsup><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\lambda\\approx\\lambda_0^2\\Delta\\nu/c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDispersive propagation modifies relative spectral phase and may\nintroduce temporal chirp.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>ν<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>π<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>≃<\/mo><mn>0.441<\/mn><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">(transform-limited Gaussian pulse)<\/mtext><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t_{\\mathrm{FWHM}}\\,\\Delta\\nu_{\\mathrm{FWHM}}=\\frac{2\\ln 2}{\\pi}\\simeq0.441\\quad\\text{(transform-limited Gaussian pulse)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>A transform-limited Gaussian pulse with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t=100\\,\\mathrm{fs}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>4.41<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\nu\\approx4.41\\,\\mathrm{THz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNear a carrier wavelength of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>800<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">800\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthis corresponds locally to about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>9.42<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">9.42\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nspectral width. The diagram separates temporal and spectral intensity;\nthe two horizontal axes are not interchangeable.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_302_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two separate Gaussian intensity curves versus time and frequency, with their respective FWHM widths.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two separate Gaussian intensity curves\nversus time and frequency, with their respective FWHM\nwidths.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Original model curves for transform-limited Gaussian\npulses.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Not measured laser data.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What minimum\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\nu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncorresponds to a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">50\\,\\mathrm{fs}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nGaussian pulse?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can spectral-phase chirp lengthen a pulse without changing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\nu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Does the coefficient\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.441<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.441<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\napply to a sech-shaped pulse?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">About\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8.82<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8.82\\,\\mathrm{THz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Spectral components acquire changed relative group delays or\nphases.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No. The time–bandwidth product depends on pulse shape and width\nconvention.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>This does not determine pulse energy, peak intensity or practical\ncompressibility of a laser system. Nonlinear propagation, spatiotemporal\ncoupling, residual chirp and measurement resolution modify the simple\nFourier limit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Ultrashort pulses can be coupled to electron beams and x-ray lasers.\n<a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M41&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-977-ultrafast-optics-spring-2005/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OCW – Ultrafast Optics: Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M41-L3 – Ultrashort Pulses, Time–Bandwidth and Dispersion · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M41-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M41-L4 – Free-Electron Lasers and Undulator Resonance<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M41 – Master: Photonics and Ultrafast Optics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M36: accelerators; M41-L3: pulse\nphysics; M32: XFEL instrumentation.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Compute the on-axis undulator\nresonance wavelength in an ideal electron-beam model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>A free-electron laser uses relativistic electrons and a periodic\nmagnetic field rather than a bound atomic laser transition. The\nundulator drives small transverse electron oscillations. Phase matching\nbetween emitted radiation and electron motion selects a wavelength;\nlaser gain requires additional physical processes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For an ideal planar undulator, on-axis fundamental radiation in the\nultrarelativistic limit satisfies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>K<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mi>γ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_r=\\lambda_u(1+K^2/2)/(2\\gamma^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the magnetic period,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe dimensionless undulator parameter and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>γ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe electron Lorentz factor. Off-axis radiation adds an angular\ncontribution. The resonance condition alone guarantees neither\nmicrobunching nor exponential FEL gain.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≃<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mi>γ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><msup><mi>K<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">(planar, on axis, fundamental)<\/mtext><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_r\\simeq\\frac{\\lambda_u}{2\\gamma^2}\\left(1+\\frac{K^2}{2}\\right)\\qquad\\text{(planar, on axis, fundamental)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For illustrative parameters\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.030<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_u=0.030\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=10^4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe result is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.25<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.225<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_r=2.25\\times10^{-10}\\,\\mathrm m=0.225\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDoubling\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>γ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwould quarter the resonance wavelength. The diagram shows magnetic\nperiods, electron trajectory and a schematic emitted wave.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_305_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Periodic undulator magnetic field, oscillating electron trajectory and schematic x-ray emission.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Periodic undulator magnetic field,\noscillating electron trajectory and schematic x-ray\nemission.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Original, not-to-scale FEL principle diagram; not a facility\nlayout.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith all other parameters fixed.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nscale with Lorentz factor at constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does the resonance formula not prove laser amplification?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.150<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.150\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∝<\/mo><msup><mi>γ<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_r\\propto\\gamma^{-2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Gain additionally requires field–beam coupling, beam quality and\nappropriate longitudinal phase evolution.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>Real facilities involve finite emittance, energy spread, harmonics,\nSASE or seeded operation, and nontrivial beam transport. The numerical\nexample is not an operating specification of any XFEL facility.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The resulting coherent x-rays enable diffraction experiments on small\nsamples. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M41&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.xfel.eu/facility/beamlines/index_eng.html\">European XFEL – Beamlines<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M41-L4 – Free-Electron Lasers and Undulator Resonance · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://lcls.slac.stanford.edu/instruments/cxi/experimental-methods\">SLAC LCLS – CXI Experimental Methods<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M41-L4 – Free-Electron Lasers and Undulator Resonance · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M41-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M41-L5 – Coherent X-ray Diffraction and Scattering Vectors<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M41 – Master: Photonics and Ultrafast Optics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M41-L4; M35: crystal diffraction;\nM31: inverse problems.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate the elastic\nscattering-vector magnitude and explain missing phase information in\nintensity measurements.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Coherent x-rays interfere after scattering from different locations\nin a specimen. Far-field diffraction patterns encode spatial frequencies\nof electron density. Detectors usually measure intensity rather than the\ncomplex field phase, so structural reconstruction poses an inverse\nproblem.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>Elastic scattering preserves\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\mathbf k_i|=|\\mathbf k_s|=2\\pi/\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith scattering vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒒<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf q=\\mathbf k_s-\\mathbf k_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor total scattering angle\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=4\\pi\\sin\\theta/\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the kinematic single-scattering approximation,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒒<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∝<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mover><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒒<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I(\\mathbf q)\\propto|\\widetilde\\rho(\\mathbf q)|^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe phase of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widetilde\\rho<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremains unmeasured, so coherent diffraction imaging needs further\nconstraints and algorithms.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒒<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∝<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mover><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒒<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=|\\mathbf k_s-\\mathbf k_i|=\\frac{4\\pi}{\\lambda}\\sin\\theta,\\qquad I(\\mathbf q)\\propto|\\widetilde\\rho(\\mathbf q)|^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=0.100\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>15<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=15^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\none finds\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>32.52<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q\\approx32.52\\,\\mathrm{nm^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe associated characteristic spatial scale\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi/q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.193<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.193\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure includes incident/scattered ray geometry and a synthetic\ndiffraction pattern; the latter is not a specimen reconstruction.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_044_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Incident and elastically scattered x-ray beams plus a synthetic diffraction pattern.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Incident and elastically scattered x-ray\nbeams plus a synthetic diffraction pattern.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Teaching illustration without measured detector data.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Compute\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>30<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=30^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat the same wavelength.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why do intensity detectors not directly reveal complex scattering\nfields?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does scale\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi/q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndiffer from guaranteed image resolution?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>62.83<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=62.83\\,\\mathrm{nm^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The detector measures magnitude squared; complex phase is not\nseparately recorded.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Practical resolution also depends on angular coverage, signal,\nnoise, radiation damage and reconstruction assumptions.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The Fourier relationship models kinematic coherent scattering.\nMultiple scattering, partial coherence, detector gaps, finite samples\nand radiation damage can substantially alter inversion.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Ultrafast scattering experiments often combine diffraction with\noptical excitation at controlled delays. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M41&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://lcls.slac.stanford.edu/instruments/cxi/experimental-methods\">SLAC LCLS – CXI Experimental Methods<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M41-L5 – Coherent X-ray Diffraction and Scattering Vectors · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-369-mathematical-methods-in-nanophotonics-spring-2008/pages/readings/\">MIT OCW – Mathematical Methods in Nanophotonics: Readings<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M41-L5 – Coherent X-ray Diffraction and Scattering Vectors · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M41-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M41-L6 – Pump–Probe Measurements, Timing Jitter and Instrument Response<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M41 – Master: Photonics and Ultrafast Optics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M41-L3; M41-L5; M32: pump–probe\ninstrumentation.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Combine pump, probe and jitter\nwidths into an idealized temporal instrument response.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>In pump–probe measurements, a pump pulse launches a transient state\nand a delayed probe records its response. Repeated measurements scan the\ndelay. Temporal resolution is not simply the duration of the shorter\npulse: both pulse envelopes and their arrival-time jitter\ncontribute.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For independent Gaussian pump and probe intensity profiles with\nindependent Gaussian timing jitter, the temporal cross-correlation is\nagain Gaussian. Variances add. Because the conversion from Gaussian\nstandard deviation to FWHM is the same for all three, their FWHM widths\nmay also be combined in quadrature. This is not generally valid for\nasymmetric shapes or correlated jitter.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≃<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msubsup><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msubsup><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msubsup><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">(independent Gaussian FWHM terms)<\/mtext><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t_{\\mathrm{IRF}}\\simeq\\sqrt{\\Delta t_{\\mathrm{pump}}^2+\\Delta t_{\\mathrm{probe}}^2+\\Delta t_{\\mathrm{jitter}}^2}\\quad\\text{(independent Gaussian FWHM terms)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>With pump FWHM of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">50\\,\\mathrm{fs}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nprobe FWHM of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>70<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">70\\,\\mathrm{fs}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand independent jitter of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">30\\,\\mathrm{fs}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\none obtains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>8300<\/mn><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>91.1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t_{\\mathrm{IRF}}=\\sqrt{8300}\\,\\mathrm{fs}\\approx91.1\\,\\mathrm{fs}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure sketches excitation and delayed probing alongside a broadened\ninstrument response.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_227_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Pump and probe pulses with variable delay and a Gaussian instrument response.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Pump and probe pulses with variable delay\nand a Gaussian instrument response.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Original idealized timing schematic.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>No facility measurement.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the width when timing jitter vanishes?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why not simply add the pulse durations arithmetically?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">When does quadratic addition of FWHM widths fail?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>50<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mn>70<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>86.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{50^2+70^2}\\approx86.0\\,\\mathrm{fs}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Independent Gaussian contributions convolve, adding variances rather\nthan widths.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Non-Gaussian shapes, correlations or systematic timing errors\nrequire a different response model.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>This model is not an empirical timing budget. Nonlinear sample\ndynamics, instabilities, pulse chirp, synchronization and shot-resolved\ncorrections can shape the measured signal.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The module closes with spatial control of photonic fields using\nperiodic media. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M41&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-977-ultrafast-optics-spring-2005/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OCW – Ultrafast Optics: Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M41-L6 – Pump–Probe Measurements, Timing Jitter and Instrument Response · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6510196/\">LCLS – Pump–Probe Experimental Methodology<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M41-L6 – Pump–Probe Measurements, Timing Jitter and Instrument Response · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://lcls.slac.stanford.edu/instruments/cxi/experimental-methods\">SLAC LCLS – CXI Experimental Methods<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M41-L6 – Pump–Probe Measurements, Timing Jitter and Instrument Response · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M41-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M41-L7 – Photonic Crystals, Periodic Index and Bragg Band Gaps<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M41 – Master: Photonics and Ultrafast Optics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M41-L1: modes; M41-L2: resonance;\nM35: Bloch states.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Estimate a simplified Bragg\nwavelength from period and effective index.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>A photonic crystal has a spatially periodic dielectric structure.\nAlong a periodic direction the fields admit Bloch-mode descriptions.\nNear Brillouin-zone boundaries, the periodic modulation can couple\ncounterpropagating waves. With sufficiently effective index contrast,\nregions of reduced or forbidden propagation may emerge.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For a simple weakly modulated one-dimensional Bragg structure, the\ncenter of first-order backscattering occurs approximately at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>β<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\beta\\approx2\\pi/a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUsing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta\\approx2\\pi n_{\\mathrm{eff}}/\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_B\\approx2n_{\\mathrm{eff}}a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe formula estimates a Bragg-resonance center, not an actual band-gap\nwidth or even proof that a full photonic band gap exists. Those depend\non contrast, polarization, geometry and modal coupling.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>β<\/mi><mo>≃<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>a<\/mi><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mo>⇒<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≃<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\beta\\simeq\\frac{2\\pi}{a}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\lambda_B\\simeq2n_{\\mathrm{eff}}a.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>250<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=250\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.50<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_{\\mathrm{eff}}=1.50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>750<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_B\\approx750\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf the period increases to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>300<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">300\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat the same assumed effective index, the estimate becomes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>900<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">900\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure connects real-space periodicity with schematic dispersion\nsplitting near a zone boundary.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_215_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Periodic refractive-index layers and schematic splitting of dispersion branches at the zone boundary.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Periodic refractive-index layers and\nschematic splitting of dispersion branches at the zone\nboundary.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Original conceptual graphic, not a computed band\nstructure.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=200\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_{\\mathrm{eff}}=1.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which physical parameters affect band-gap width?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does the Bragg wavelength alone not establish a complete\nthree-dimensional gap?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>600<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">600\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For instance index contrast, geometry, polarization and spatial mode\ncoupling.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It considers only one idealized periodic direction and a simplified\npair of modes.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>Strong modulation, multidimensional crystals, anisotropic media and\ndispersion require complete Maxwell eigenvalue calculations. This\nillustration does not establish a band gap or replace optical\ntransmission measurements.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>M41 concludes the photonics sequence from guided modes and ultrashort\npulses to periodic structures.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-46-photonic-materials-and-devices-spring-2006/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OCW – Photonic Materials and Devices: Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M41-L7 – Photonic Crystals, Periodic Index and Bragg Band Gaps · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-369-mathematical-methods-in-nanophotonics-spring-2008/pages/readings/\">MIT OCW – Mathematical Methods in Nanophotonics: Readings<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M41-L7 – Photonic Crystals, Periodic Index and Bragg Band Gaps · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
